# Cisco VLAN and 802.1Q Trunking Lab

For this lab, I wanted to do basically the same configuration as my last lab, but add 802.1Q encapsulation and configure trunk ports on the switch.

This lab consisted of:

* [ ] Wipe configuration
* [ ] Configure hostname
* [ ] Disable DNS lookup
* [ ] Configure enable secret
* [ ] Configure console access
* [ ] Configure SSH
* [ ] Configure management VLAN
* [ ] Configure management IP
* [ ] Create VLANs
* [ ] Configure access ports
* [ ] Configure trunk ports
* [ ] Configure 802.1Q trunking
* [ ] Configure port security
* [ ] Disable unused ports
* [ ] Save configuration
* [ ] Verify configuration
* [ ] Troubleshoot problems
* [ ] Wipe switch

I'm going to skip the initial configuration part of this lab documentation. I mostly wanted this lab to focus on VLAN configuration and 802.1Q trunking.

## 1. VLAN Configuration

Several VLANs were created to separate different types of network traffic.

| VLAN | Name       |
| ---- | ---------- |
| 10   | Home       |
| 20   | Lab        |
| 30   | Test       |
| 99   | Management |
| 250  | Blackhole  |

The VLANs were created and named using:

cisco
vlan 10
 name Home

vlan 20
 name Lab

vlan 30
 name Test

vlan 99
 name Management

vlan 250
 name Blackhole


The VLAN configuration was verified with:

cisco
show vlan


The command confirmed that VLANs 10, 20, 30, 99, and 250 were active.

## 2. Access Port Configuration

The switch ports were configured as access ports and assigned to the appropriate VLANs.

### VLAN 10

Ports Gi0/1 through Gi0/24 were assigned to VLAN 10.

cisco
interface range gi0/1 - 24
 switchport mode access
 switchport access vlan 10


### VLAN 20

Ports Gi0/25 through Gi0/35 were assigned to VLAN 20.

cisco
interface range gi0/25 - 35
 switchport mode access
 switchport access vlan 20


### VLAN 30

Ports Gi0/36 through Gi0/48 were assigned to VLAN 30.

cisco
interface range gi0/36 - 48
 switchport mode access
 switchport access vlan 30


I confirmed the VLAN assignments with:

cisco
show vlan


Most of this configuration was just for practice. I wanted to make sure every GigabitEthernet port was assigned to a VLAN.

## 3. Port Security

On all of the access ports, I configured sticky MAC addresses. This allows the switch to learn the MAC address of the device connected to the port.

If another device is connected to that port, the switch can treat it as a security violation depending on the port-security configuration.

Example:

cisco
interface gi0/25
 switchport mode access
 switchport access vlan 20
 switchport port-security mac-address sticky


