## 4. Unused Port Security

I shut down ports that I considered unused for the sake of practice.

The unused ports were also assigned to the Blackhole VLAN.

## 5. Trunk Port Configuration

TenGigabitEthernet1/1 and TenGigabitEthernet1/2 were configured as 802.1Q trunk ports.

The ports were configured using:

cisco
interface TenGigabitEthernet1/1
 switchport trunk encapsulation dot1q
 switchport mode trunk


The switch was configured to use 802.1Q encapsulation for trunking.

At this point, I do not have any other network devices connected to the switch. Because of this, the command:

cisco
show interfaces trunk


does not return any interfaces.

This was expected because the command displays interfaces that are currently operating as trunks.

I verified the configuration with:

cisco
show running-config interface te1/1


The output showed:

cisco
interface TenGigabitEthernet1/1
 switchport trunk encapsulation dot1q
 switchport mode trunk


Hopefully, in the near future I will connect the switch to another network device and verify an operational 802.1Q trunk.

## 6. Troubleshooting and Lessons Learned

One of the issues I encountered in this lab was when I tried to configure Te1/1 and Te1/2 as trunk ports. I received an error stating that a dynamic port could not be configured as a trunk port, or something similar.

I had encountered this before in my last lab, but I did not remember how to fix it.

This time, I configured the 802.1Q encapsulation first. I believe this is related to this older Cisco switch requiring the trunk encapsulation to be selected before configuring the port as a trunk.

I also made a couple of mistakes by not initially setting which VLANs were allowed across the trunk and by not setting the native VLAN. I later added a description to the trunk port as well.

The commands I used to configure these settings were:

cisco
switchport trunk native vlan 99
switchport trunk allowed vlan 10,20,30,99


Another mistake I made was adding the sticky MAC address command to my trunk port. I learned that port security is not configured on a trunk port in the same way it is on an access port.

To remove the sticky MAC configuration, I used:

cisco
no switchport port-security mac-address sticky


The last command I added was:

cisco
switchport nonegotiate


I added this mostly for practice since I was manually configuring the interface as a trunk port.

## Conclusion

This lab gave me some good hands-on experience configuring a Cisco switch.

I configured the switch with multiple VLANs, access ports, SSH access, basic port security, unused-port security, and 802.1Q trunk interfaces.

The configuration was saved to the startup configuration and verified using Cisco IOS `show` commands.

The next phase of the lab will be to connect the switch to another network device and verify trunk operation between the devices.


